{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795429836,"nonce":"s7tr9nuUbunDSzbLlpOjRNYrEUQ=","title":"Python: a parameter called with a lambda and then a module function segfaults","message":"Release zypy at e054c15e exits 139 (SIGSEGV) after the first call returns. A parameter that receives a lambda at one call and a module function at another is the shape. CPython prints \"caught 7\", \"shown\", \"caught 5\", \"shown\", \"end\".\n\n    def show(f):\n        try:\n            f()\n        except KeyError as e:\n            print(\"caught\", e)\n        print(\"shown\")\n\n    d = {1: 2}\n    show(lambda: d[7])\n\n    def deleter():\n        del d[5]\n\n    show(deleter)\n    print(\"end\")\n\nzypy prints \"caught 7\" and \"shown\", then dies with status 139; no error text. The output is the same without the KeyError text change that was pending when this was found, so it is not caused by it. Related: bdddb0b (callee sets for parameters that receive module functions or closures). Check the closure and module-function call-through-a-parameter path, and what the call reads when the record is a closure but the target is a bare function.","files":null}]}